 ## Overview
-Surface shading for thresholded volumes applies lighting models to isosurfaces extracted from scalar volumes by determining surface geometry and illumination in image space or via rasterization hardware, distinct from direct volume rendering shading [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Deferred Shading for Isosurfaces](867-deferred-shading-for-isosurfaces.md) by avoiding the high memory and computational costs of per-ray cell storage or repeated neighbor-finding, often relying on z-buffer slopes or precomputed gradients rather than deferred multi-pass normal accumulation [S2, unofficial][S3].
+Surface shading for thresholded volumes applies lighting models to isosurfaces extracted from scalar volumes by determining surface geometry and illumination in image space or via rasterization hardware [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Deferred Shading for Isosurfaces](867-deferred-shading-for-isosurfaces.md) by explicitly avoiding deferred normal computation to prevent prohibitive memory and computational costs associated with per-ray cell storage or repeated neighbor-finding [S2, unofficial][S3].
 
 ## How it works
-The method determines surface appearance for pixels where a ray intersects an isosurface defined by a threshold $f(X) = c$. The core challenge is estimating the surface normal $\mathbf{n}$ and applying a lighting model, typically Lambertian, without the overhead of full deferred shading.
